WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:04.670
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Skillshare, home to over 20,000

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:07.160
classes that could teach you a new life skill.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:10.320
Last month MIT revealed their ion propelled
plane.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.669
The product of 7 years of development and
the first of its kind.

00:00:13.669 --> 00:00:18.120
A plane capable of sustained powered flight
with no moving parts in it’s propulsion

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.220
system.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:23.331
Just as the Wright Brothers announced to the
world that powered flight was possible, this

00:00:23.331 --> 00:00:27.980
flight lays down a milestone for ion drive
technology that could pave the way to future

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.170
investment and development.

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:33.760
It has the potential to drastically improve
propulsion technology.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.370
Having no moving parts is a benefit that cannot
be understated.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:41.400
Parts can be made lighter as they no longer
need to survive the stress of movement.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:45.810
Reduced stress means reduced maintenance and
costs, but perhaps the most immediate benefit

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:49.310
we can garner from this technology is reduced
noise.

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:54.390
With no noisy combustion or rotating aerodynamic
surfaces stirring up the air, these planes

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:55.620
are like gliding owls.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:59.920
A characteristic military contractors will
be eager to take advantage of.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.809
But with current limitations, this may take
some time to come to market.

00:01:03.809 --> 00:01:08.220
Let’s investigate just how this new technology
works, and where it needs to improve in order

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.650
to compete with current technology.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:15.490
This technology has been in development for
decades now with many spacecraft already using

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:20.350
variations on the idea to achieve highly efficient
thrust systems.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:25.060
These engines work on a similar principle
to the ion propulsion of the MIT plane, albeit

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:28.940
in a very different environment that lends
itself to the technology.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.280
Take the NSTAR ion drive aboard the now retired
Dawn spacecraft.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.020
This spacecraft used xenon as a propellant,
because it has a high atomic mass allowing

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:43.490
it to provide more kick per atom, while being
inert and having a high storage density lending

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:48.590
itself to long term storage on a spacecraft.[1]
The engine releases both xenon atoms and high

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:54.159
energy electrons into the ionization chamber,
where they collide to produce a positive xenon

00:01:54.159 --> 00:01:56.090
atom and more electrons.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:00.509
These electrons are then collected by the
positively charged chamber walls, while the

00:02:00.509 --> 00:02:04.979
positive xenon atoms migrate towards the chamber
exit which contains two grids.

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:10.030
A positive grid called the screen grid, and
a negative grid called the accelerator grid.

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:15.280
The high electrical potential between these
grids causes the positive ions to accelerate

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.830
and shoot out of the engine at speeds up to
145, 000 kilometres per hour.

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:25.890
At that speed even the tiny xenon atoms can
provide a decent bit of thrust, but even still

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:30.510
this engine provides a maximum of 92 milli
Newtons of force.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:34.830
About the same force a piece of paper will
exert while resting on your hand.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:39.590
But in the vacuum of space there is no air
to sap away the precious energy we provide.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:44.190
With no drag or friction to remove energy
we gradually build up our kinetic energy and

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:45.320
gain speed.

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:52.450
The dawn spacecraft weighed about 1220 kilograms
at launch with a dry mass of 750 kilograms

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:56.560
after the propellant had been expended, so
lets say it has an average weight between

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.959
the two of 1000 kilograms.

00:02:58.959 --> 00:03:03.930
Rearranging the force equals mass by acceleration
equation, we can calculate the acceleration

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:10.440
this engine could provide at 0.000092 metres
per second squared.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:15.239
A tiny acceleration, but multiple by a week
(604800 seconds) and our spacecraft is flying

00:03:15.239 --> 00:03:17.599
at 55.6 m/s.

00:03:17.599 --> 00:03:24.379
Multiple it by a year and it’s flying at
2898 metres per second, that’s 8.5 mach.

00:03:24.379 --> 00:03:29.620
The latest generation ion drives, dubbed the
NEXT engine, can produce three times the force

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:34.210
and has been tested continuously without stopping
for 6 years straight here on earth.

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:42.880
That’s enough force to accelerate that 1000
kilograms to 44651 m/s, 130 times the speed

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:43.880
of sound.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:44.880
[1]

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:48.420
This is an incredible technology, that will
revolutionise how we explore space in the

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:53.269
near future, but here on earth it has a completely
different set of challenges.

00:03:53.269 --> 00:03:56.570
Here on earth planes pose a completely different
challenge.

00:03:56.570 --> 00:04:01.459
Air will continuously sap away any energy
we input into our vehicle through drag, and

00:04:01.459 --> 00:04:06.530
so we need to create an ion drive that can
provide more energy than air can remove while

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:09.330
travelling fast enough to achieve flight.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:13.530
Not an easy task and the fact MIT have managed
it is mind blowing.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:15.330
Let’s see how they did it.

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:18.619
They first needed to optimize their plane
design for the application.

00:04:18.619 --> 00:04:23.979
Reducing weight to minimize the energy required
to maintain height, and minimising drag to

00:04:23.979 --> 00:04:27.009
reduce any energy losses to the air.

00:04:27.009 --> 00:04:31.999
They did this using something called geometric
programming optimization, which allows designers

00:04:31.999 --> 00:04:37.330
to specify constraints and design criteria
to a programme which will then find the optimal

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:38.330
design.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:39.330
[2]

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:42.680
After running multiple computer simulations
they settled on a plane with a 5 metre wingspan

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:45.490
and a weight of 2.56 kilograms.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:51.300
It would require a flight speed of 4.8 metres
per second with a thrust of 3.2 Newtons [3].

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:56.889
3.2 Newtons is vastly more than anything achieved
by NSTAR or NEXT engines, but they don’t

00:04:56.889 --> 00:04:59.020
work in entirely the same way.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:04.099
Ion drives for space need to carry atoms to
be bombarded, within earth's atmosphere there

00:05:04.099 --> 00:05:09.379
is no shortage of atoms to ionize and accelerate
and this helps counteract some of the negatives

00:05:09.379 --> 00:05:11.930
of the drag they also induce.

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:16.199
The planes propulsion comes from an array
of ion drives carried below the wing.

00:05:16.199 --> 00:05:21.529
The positive anode was a thin steel wire,
which helped minimise the drag it induced.

00:05:21.529 --> 00:05:26.830
While the cathodes were foam aerofoils covered
in thin aluminium, these being light and capable

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:29.789
of producing lift to offset their weight.

00:05:29.789 --> 00:05:34.781
In this case nitrogen is ionized and attracted
across the electric field induced by the 20

00:05:34.781 --> 00:05:37.789
thousand volts of electric potential between
them.

00:05:37.789 --> 00:05:42.690
The nitrogen ions collide with neutral air
molecules along the way to a provide additional

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:43.719
thrust.

00:05:43.719 --> 00:05:46.110
Creating something called ionic wind.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:50.740
Getting that 20 thousand volts of alternating
current is really the most difficult part

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:55.460
and the team had to design their own lightweight
high-power voltage converter to step-up the

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:59.879
200 volts of direct current drawn from their
lithium polymer batteries.

00:05:59.879 --> 00:06:04.530
This energy storage conundrum, as explained
in my electric planes video, is the biggest

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:07.629
challenge facing any technology like this.

00:06:07.629 --> 00:06:12.979
So how does this compare to conventional propulsion
methods regarding thrust to power ratios?

00:06:12.979 --> 00:06:17.870
A typical jet engine achieves a thrust to
power ratio of 3 Newtons per kilowatt, while

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:22.379
helicopter rotors achieve a power to thrust
ratio of about 50 Newtons per kilowatt (N

00:06:22.379 --> 00:06:23.379
kw-1).

00:06:23.379 --> 00:06:28.110
This ion propelled plane is estimated to have
achieved a thrust to power ratio of 6.25 Newtons

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:29.110
per kilowatt.

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:34.060
So, if we could find a way of powering these
devices that didn’t require heavy batteries,

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:36.729
could these ion propulsion engines be used?

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:37.729
[3]

00:06:37.729 --> 00:06:42.389
Scaling these propulsion method is not easy,
and individual electrode pairs have their

00:06:42.389 --> 00:06:47.619
limit in the current they can pass between
them via ion transport, due to limits in voltage

00:06:47.619 --> 00:06:51.869
and choked flow within the electric field
[4], just as air can become choked within

00:06:51.869 --> 00:06:53.309
a constricted pipe.

00:06:53.309 --> 00:06:57.959
This affects something called the thrust density,
which is the area over which the thrust is

00:06:57.959 --> 00:06:59.159
applied.

00:06:59.159 --> 00:07:04.149
Jet Engines have a very high thrust density
at over 10,000 Newtons per metres squared,

00:07:04.149 --> 00:07:08.119
so we can produce a great about of force with
relatively low area.

00:07:08.119 --> 00:07:12.629
This plane achieved a thrust density of 3
Newtons per metre squared, so it is generating

00:07:12.629 --> 00:07:15.879
very little force over a very large area.

00:07:15.879 --> 00:07:20.819
We can just about manage to provide enough
force with 4 3 racks and 2 rows of these electrodes

00:07:20.819 --> 00:07:24.889
hanging far below the wing, for a plane this
light and slow.

00:07:24.889 --> 00:07:29.419
The issue here is the same issue that prevents
batteries from being a viable solution for

00:07:29.419 --> 00:07:34.170
planes, power requirements do not scale linearly
with the mass of the plane, they increase

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:35.879
with the square of the mass.

00:07:35.879 --> 00:07:40.319
While the power requirements to overcome drag
increases with the cube of the velocity.

00:07:40.319 --> 00:07:45.400
So our ion propulsion power will need to scale
with it, but we cannot simply hang racks and

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:47.729
racks of these electrodes beneath our plane.

00:07:47.729 --> 00:07:52.169
They, along with the structures required to
support them would cause far too much drag,

00:07:52.169 --> 00:07:56.139
and in turn flight surfaces would need to
scale to counteract the pitching moment this

00:07:56.139 --> 00:07:58.669
would cause, causing even more drag.

00:07:58.669 --> 00:08:03.080
This technology is still in its infancy and
there are engineers far more intelligent than

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:06.439
I working to figure out ways to apply it.

00:08:06.439 --> 00:08:12.399
Just think that 114 years ago the Wright Flyer
managed to fly just 35 metres in it’s 11

00:08:12.399 --> 00:08:14.159
second first flight.

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:19.059
This ion propelled plane managed 55 metres
in 12 seconds, and who knows where we will

00:08:19.059 --> 00:08:21.069
be in 100 years time.

00:08:21.069 --> 00:08:25.759
We as a species are continually growing and
learning how to apply these principles.

00:08:25.759 --> 00:08:31.400
It’s a fundamental part of the human experience
to continuously develop your skillset, just

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:35.750
as I have been developing my segue skills
by watching this course by wendover productions

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:38.839
